```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class HtmlParser {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body>Hello, World!</body></html>";
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(htmlString);

        // Extract the title from the HTML
        Element title = doc.title();
        if (title != null) {
            System.out.println("Title: " + title.data());
        } else {
            System.out.println("No title found.");
        }

        // Extract all links on the page
        Elements links = doc.select("a");
        for (Element link : links) {
            System.out.println(link.attr("href"));
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we use Jsoup to parse an HTML string and extract the title and all links from the document. The `Jsoup.parse()` method is used to parse the HTML string into a Document object, which can be used to navigate and search through the HTML content.

You can also use Jsoup to create a new HTML page or modify an existing one:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class HtmlCreator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String htmlString = "<html><body>Hello, World!</body></html>";
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(htmlString);

        // Create a new paragraph and add it to the page
        Element p = doc.createElement("p");
        p.text("This is a new paragraph.");
        doc.body().appendChild(p);

        // Write the modified HTML to a file
        String writer = mock(StringWriter.class);
        when(writer.appendLine).thenReturn(writer);
        System.out.println(doc.html());
    }
}

class StringWriter {
    private StringBuilder builder;

    public StringWriter() {
        builder = new StringBuilder();
    }

    public void appendLine(String line) {
        builder.append(line).append("\n");
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return builder.toString();
    }
}
```

This example uses Jsoup to create a new HTML document and add a new paragraph to it. The `StringWriter` class is used to write the modified HTML to a string, which can be written to a file.

Please note that you will need to have Jsoup installed in your project to use this code. You can add it to your project using Maven or Gradle:

Maven:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jsoup</groupId>
    <artifactId>jsoup</artifactId>
    <version>1.14.3</version>
</dependency>
```

Gradle:
```groovy
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.jsoup:jsoup:1.14.3'
}
```
